- How to validate accuracy on real data?
- How would this adapt if k changes dynamically?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming algorithm intuition and amortized reasoning. Detecting heavy hitters with bounded memory on a growing stream is a common interview topic; Misra–Gries provides deterministic guarantees with simple updates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra–Gries heavy-hitters
- Choice of k = floor(1/phi) + 1 to bound missed items
- Amortized O(1) per element, O(k) memory
- Trade-offs: larger k reduces false positives but uses more memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class MGHeavyHitters {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  update(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
      return;
    }
    if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
      return;
    }
    for (const [key, val] of Array.from(this.counters)) {
      if (val === 1) this.counters.delete(key);
      else this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
    }
  }
  candidates() { return Array.from(this.counters.keys()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the phi-bound with a second pass or a secondary sketch in production?
- How does batch size B influence practical update throughput and memory usage?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming analytics under fixed memory, focusing on real-time percentile estimation and how rebinding affects accuracy and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms, fixed memory, boundary recomputation, percentile estimation, amortized analysis.
- Trade-offs between K, R, and accuracy; cache-friendly bucket access.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Histogram85 {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M; this.K = K;
    this.bounds = Array.from({length: K}, (_,i)=> Math.floor((i+1)*M/K));
    this.counts = new Array(K).fill(0);
  }
  update(x){
    let bucket = Math.min(this.K-1, Math.floor(x/ (this.M/this.K)));
    this.counts[bucket]++;
  }
  total(){ return this.counts.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0); }
  percentile(p){ // p in [0,1]
    const total = this.total();
    const target = p * total;
    let acc = 0;
    for(let i=this.K-1; i>=0; i--){
      acc += this.counts[i];
      if(acc >= target) return this.bounds[i-1] !== undefined ? this.bounds[i-1] : this.M;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

